  "transcribed_text": " Four GPUs can serve independent requests  or participate in one synchronous training group.  The device count matches, but the dependencies differ.  In this illustrative serving example,  three requests finish at 100 milliseconds.  The fourth takes 140.  The first three can return without waiting for the fourth.  Now consider the training group.  Three participants are ready after 100 milliseconds,  but the fourth needs another 40.  The next operation requires all four,  so the early participants wait.  We're isolating this dependency.  Communication duration and overlapping work aren't included.  Three GPUs each wait 40 milliseconds.  That's 120 GPU milliseconds of aggregate waiting time.  A GPU millisecond means one GPU waiting for one millisecond here.  The group's elapsed delay is 40 milliseconds, not 120.  An independent job outside this group can continue.  A fifth GPU doesn't automatically supply  the missing contribution.  Before adding devices, identify which operation needs which participants.  A delay can reduce useful throughput  without corrupting the training state  or stopping every service in the data center.",
